        {
          "source_quote": "οὐ θέλεις τὴν σὴν ὑφ’ ἑτέρου μοιχευθῆναι γυναῖκα, τὴν ἑτέρου μὴ μοίχευε γαμετήν",
          "english_quote": "You do not want your wife to be violated by another through adultery; do not commit adultery with another man’s wedded wife.",
          "issue": "In modern English, a woman being “violated by another” suggests rape or sexual coercion. μοιχευθῆναι specifies adultery with the wife, without specifying coercion. The qualification “through adultery” does not adequately remove the added implication. The same Greek verb is also less transparently represented in the first half than in the reciprocal prohibition.",
          "suggested_revision": "Proposed for fresh review: “You do not want another to commit adultery with your wife; do not commit adultery with another man’s wedded wife.” This changes the English voice while preserving the participants, negations, adultery terminology, and distinction between γυναῖκα and γαμετήν.",
